From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 12:02:56 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(chunks): load chunks that arrive just before a teleport

Some servers send the chunk at a teleport destination before the teleport
and the network chunk publisher update that move the player there. On join,
DonutSMP sends the player's own chunk after a publisher update centered on
the previous position, then teleports the player about 8,000 blocks away and
sends the next publisher update. ViaBedrock checked the chunk against the old
position, dropped it, and the server never sent it again. Java cannot build
the visible world around a camera whose own chunk is missing, so the whole
world and its entities stayed invisible.

The level chunk handler now keeps a chunk that is outside the load range as
raw packet bytes. The next center update replays the kept chunks that are now
in range through the normal handler and forgets the rest, so the buffer holds
at most the chunks of one publisher interval. A bound of 64 chunks keeps a
misbehaving server from growing it.

Evidence: a recorded DonutSMP join (protocol 2225) sends chunk -3665,13985
between a publisher update for -3152,13626 and the teleport to
-58626.5 97.6 223765.5. With the old handler, the replay rejected exactly
that chunk and sent the other 196 to Java while the world stayed empty.
Native Bedrock players spawn into a visible world on the same server.

